    "title": "Is there mail over Easter weekend?",
    "text": "Easter Sunday falls this year on April 5. While there won't be any postal service on the day itself, many customers are wondering if mail will still be delivered on the surrounding holidays. Here’s what to know about services from the U.S. Postal Service (USPS), UPS, and FedEx during the Easter holiday period in 2026.Is Mail Delivered on Good Friday?This year, Good Friday falls on April 3. Good Friday is not an official federal holiday, so USPS mail delivery will follow its regular schedule. Letters and packages will be delivered as usual, and local post offices will remain open for normal operations.Good Friday commemorates the crucifixion of Jesus Christ and is observed by many Christians on the Friday before Easter.Regardless of their origins, UPS and FedEx are expected to operate on their typical schedules on Good Friday, with most services unaffected. FedEx may have some modifications for specific services, so checking with a local branch is advised.A United States Postal Service van and truck parked outside a USPS location in Houston, Texas, on March 31, 2026. | Aaron M. Sprecher via APIs Mail Delivered on Easter Monday?Easter Monday, which falls on April 6, 2026, is treated as a regular business day by USPS, UPS, and FedEx because it is not a federal holiday. Mail delivery and post office hours will proceed without interruption.Is the Post Office Open?Yes. Post offices are open on Good Friday (April 3) and Easter Monday (April 6), operating under standard business hours. There's no post on Sundays, and Easter is no exception: USPS post offices will be closed, and regular mail delivery will not occur. Not to fret: Priority Mail Express, the only USPS service active on Sundays, will still be available for urgent shipments. UPS and FedEx are also closed on Easter Sunday, suspending most shipping and pickup services. Their normal operations are expected to resume the following Monday. For more details about USPS holiday closures, check the official USPS holiday schedule or carrier-specific updates from UPS and FedEx.",
